            {
              "id": "87840232-3a4f-379f-883b-e52b757685ad",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that would dereference the pointer. The absence of a null guard before use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. These factors confirm this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning from the function, logging an error, or cleaning up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, cleanup, etc.)\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that memset is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "32c1eeca-fc40-3613-bab8-79c7ecb55844",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', which indicates a high-confidence issue where the result of malloc is used without verifying if the allocation succeeded. The code assigns the result of malloc(size) to hData, and the first use is as the destination in a memset call, which will dereference the pointer. There is no null guard present before this use, increasing the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and also passed to another call, but these do not address the immediate risk of dereferencing a potentially null pointer. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the pointer. For example, after:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\n```\nadd a null check before using `hData`:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hData, 0, size);\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "105928f6-02ea-38d2-9845-efbedf2e1edb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is assigned to 'hOutputData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the allocation size is not a simple sizeof(T) pattern, making it harder to reason about safety. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not address the immediate risk of dereferencing a NULL pointer if malloc fails. The call verdict is 'GENUINE', confirming this is a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning from the function, logging an error, or cleaning up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, cleanup, etc.)\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that memset is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
