              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is assigned to 'hData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that would dereference the pointer. The absence of a null check before use makes this a genuine risk for null dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not affect the initial dereference risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the pointer. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before using `hData`:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, cleanup, etc.)\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
