                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', which indicates a high-confidence issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData', but there is no null check before the first use of this pointer in a memset call. The absence of a zero guard or overflow guard further increases the risk, as does the immediate use of the pointer as a destination in a memory operation. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. Although the pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, these do not address the core issue of a potential null dereference. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability where a failed allocation could lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the pointer. For example, after:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\n```\nadd a null check before using `hOutputData`:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to use the null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
