              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(size) and assigns the result to hData, but there is no null pointer check before the first use of hData as the destination in a memset call. This means that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the subsequent memset will dereference a null pointer, leading to undefined behavior or a crash. There are no zero guards, overflow guards, or null guards present before the first use, and the first use is a memory operation (memset), which is considered dangerous without a null check. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, but these do not affect the immediate risk of null dereference.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, after allocating memory with `hData = (type*)malloc(size);`, add a null check before using `hData`:\n\n```c\nhData = (type*)malloc(size);\nif (hData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hData, 0, size);\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails, the program will not attempt to dereference a null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
