            {
              "id": "9eb74e37-0361-3232-a5ed-ee419213e6d9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which does not check the boundaries of the buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include boundary checks. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For example, instead of `memmove()`, you can use `std::copy()` or `std::copy_backward()` from `<algorithm>` library, which are safer as they work with STL containers that have boundary checks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove()` function with `std::copy()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <sys/socket.h>\n\n// ...\n\nstruct cmsghdr* cmptr;\nint sendfd;\n\n// ...\n\nstd::copy(&sendfd, &sendfd + sizeof(sendfd), CMSG_DATA(cmptr));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` or `<cstring>` for `memmove()`\n- `<sys/socket.h>` for `CMSG_DATA()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "96ab1af4-ba3b-370c-935b-0c88ab840e6d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to serious security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cliaddr.sun_path, temp, sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1);\ncliaddr.sun_path[sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code ensures that no more than `sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1` bytes are copied from `temp` to `cliaddr.sun_path`, and that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` for the `strncpy` function\n- `<sys/un.h>` for the `sockaddr_un` structure\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "002235c5-6e6e-368c-b1c1-b35094b937cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(handle->name, name, sizeof(handle->name) - 1);\nhandle->name[sizeof(handle->name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `name` to `handle->name`, but it will not copy more than `sizeof(handle->name) - 1` characters. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "779ac4f0-aca5-3ea5-8f6e-d700e344e9fb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(handle->name, name, sizeof(handle->name) - 1);\nhandle->name[sizeof(handle->name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `name` to `handle->name`, but it will not copy more than `sizeof(handle->name) - 1` characters. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
