                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: I