            {
              "id": "18c78b27-661b-3cd6-a094-636519d3f6d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to a potential buffer overflow or underflow. In the given code snippet, `malloc(N * sizeof(float))`, if the value of `N` is not properly controlled, it can lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller buffer being allocated than expected. This can lead to subsequent buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the buffer is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input values that are used to calculate the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated to prevent integer overflows.\n3. Consider using languages that automatically manage memory for you to prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the given code snippet would be to check if the multiplication operation would result in an overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n\nif (N > 0 && N <= (INT_MAX / sizeof(float))) {\n    float* array = (float*) malloc(N * sizeof(float));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use array\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `N` is within a safe range to prevent an integer overflow. Then, we check if `malloc` was successful in allocating the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `malloc` function and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd379c8e-e8e3-339c-b3a2-fc103682ea7f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to a potential buffer overflow or underflow. In the given code snippet, `malloc(N * sizeof(float))`, if the value of `N` is not properly controlled, it can lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller buffer being allocated than expected. This can lead to subsequent buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the buffer is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input values that are used to calculate the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated to prevent integer overflows.\n3. Consider using languages that automatically manage memory for you to prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the given code snippet would be to check if the multiplication operation would result in an overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n\nif (N > 0 && N <= (INT_MAX / sizeof(float))) {\n    float* array = (float*) malloc(N * sizeof(float));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use array\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `N` is within a safe range to prevent an integer overflow. Then, we check if `malloc` was successful in allocating the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `malloc` function and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
