            {
              "id": "968ac24b-3833-3ab0-9d4d-4c93a6f05cab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `layerSize` parameter passed to `malloc` is controlled by the user or can be manipulated in some way, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n- Always validate and sanitize input that will be used as the size parameter for `malloc`.\n- Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns NULL, it means that the memory allocation failed. Failing to check this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash your program.\n- Always free the memory that you have allocated with `malloc` once you are done with it to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\n// Assuming layerSize is an integer\nif (layerSize > 0 && layerSize <= MAX_LAYER_SIZE) {\n    void* layer = malloc(layerSize);\n    if (layer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use layer\n    free(layer);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `layerSize` is within an acceptable range. Then we check if `malloc` was successful before using the allocated memory. Finally, we free the memory once we are done with it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "72565f51-0c16-34cd-bdc7-725a2584e1e0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for `memsize` that can cause the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, which returns a null pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the `malloc(memsize)` vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (memsize <= 0 || memsize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: invalid size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(memsize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include `stdlib.h` to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "85965ec8-32f7-3d77-84bc-5436a9b08e86",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of the block is specified by the `size` parameter. If the allocation is successful, the function returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any data.\n\nA vulnerability can occur when the `size` parameter passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities, including buffer overflows, integer overflows, and memory corruption. These vulnerabilities can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, cause a denial of service, or leak sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and limit the `size` parameter passed to `malloc`.\n- Check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the allocation was successful.\n- Initialize the allocated memory to a known value using `memset` or similar function.\n- Use a safe version of `malloc` that includes built-in error checking and validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to safely use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size == 0 || size > SIZE_MAX) {\n        // Invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize memory to zero\n    memset(ptr, 0, size);\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: for `malloc`\n- `string.h`: for `memset`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "32a747d7-c0b5-30a5-84d8-b23d59d16efa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a memset call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. The allocation size is not a simple sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero guards or overflow checks present. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also stored in a field and passed to other calls, increasing the impact of a potential null dereference. These factors indicate a genuine risk of null pointer dereference if malloc fails, which can lead to undefined behavior or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Allocation failed for hOutputData\\n\");\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that memset is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a null pointer dereference. For more information, see:\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b20bee4e-c534-3789-8e29-1b5ac6f45797",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used (via pointer field access) without a preceding check for NULL. The first use of the allocated pointer is a potentially dangerous operation (ptr-field) and there is no null guard before this use. The malloc call is cast, but this does not mitigate the risk. There are no zero guards or overflow guards present, and the allocation size is based on sizeof a struct, which is a common and correct pattern, but does not guarantee safety if malloc fails. The pointer is also passed to another call, which could propagate the risk. These factors indicate a genuine risk of null dereference if memory allocation fails.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc for NULL before using the allocated pointer. This prevents dereferencing a null pointer if memory allocation fails. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nHIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *desc = (HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *)malloc(sizeof(HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR));\ndesc->Width = ...; // unsafe if malloc failed\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\nHIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *desc = (HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *)malloc(sizeof(HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR));\nif (desc == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or cleanup\n    return;\n}\ndesc->Width = ...; // safe to use desc\n```\n\nThis ensures that the pointer is only used if the allocation succeeded, preventing undefined behavior and potential crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9ad04f53-627e-3c4b-aafe-7b8f75bc7092",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used as the destination in a memset operation immediately after allocation, without any check to ensure that the allocation succeeded (i.e., that the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that will dereference the pointer. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also stored in a field and freed in the method, but these do not address the immediate risk of null dereference. This pattern is a common source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities, especially if malloc can return NULL due to allocation failure.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before using the pointer:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    // ...\n} else {\n    memset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
