            {
              "id": "ad335ea3-0685-38b9-af85-47ae0ad73b3b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to potential buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(void*))`, the size of the memory to be allocated is the size of a pointer, which may not be sufficient for the intended data, leading to potential overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the length of the copy operation to prevent buffer overflow.\n3. Consider using modern C++ constructs like `new` and `delete` instead of `malloc` and `free` for memory management.\n4. Use libraries and tools that can help detect and prevent such vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are trying to allocate memory for an array of `n` integers, the correct way would be:\n\n```cpp\nint* arr = (int*)malloc(n * sizeof(int));\n```\n\nIf you are using modern C++, consider using `new`:\n\n```cpp\nint* arr = new int[n];\n```\n\nAnd don't forget to free the allocated memory when you're done:\n\n```cpp\nfree(arr); // if you used malloc\ndelete[] arr; // if you used new\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-122: Heap-based Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/122.html)"
