            {
              "id": "70e66dc9-e298-319a-af7a-6803d0cdfe43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can lead to crashes, incorrect behavior, or even code execution if an attacker can control the overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string after writing to it. \n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum length of the string to read. This can prevent buffer overflows even if the string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `error_string` is not guaranteed to be null-terminated, you should use `strnlen()` instead of `strlen()`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(error_string, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` should be the maximum length of `error_string` that you expect. This way, even if `error_string` is not null-terminated, `strnlen()` will stop reading after `MAX_LENGTH` characters.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` and `strnlen()` functions are part of the C standard library, so you don't need any additional libraries to use them. However, you need to include the `string.h` header file:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5b86be9a-96fe-322c-989d-c1e5f9da0bbc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the size parameter, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking for negative values and values that are too large.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory allocation, such as `calloc()`.\n3. Handle the case where `malloc()` fails and returns a NULL pointer. This can prevent null pointer dereferences and other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to fix the vulnerability in the `malloc(sizeElements)` function:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t sizeElements) {\n    if (sizeElements <= 0 || sizeElements > MAX_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(sizeElements);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "680057fd-73f7-37b2-aaa2-e3a3e847e7d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can lead to crashes, incorrect behavior, or even code execution if an attacker can control the overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string after writing to it. \n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum length of the string to read. This can prevent buffer overflows even if the string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `error_string` is not guaranteed to be null-terminated, you should use `strnlen()` instead of `strlen()`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(error_string, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` should be the maximum length of `error_string` that you expect. This way, even if `error_string` is not null-terminated, `strnlen()` will stop reading after `MAX_LENGTH` characters.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` and `strnlen()` functions are part of the C standard library, so you don't need any additional libraries to use them. However, you need to include the `string.h` header file:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d05700c8-d1c5-33c0-bc23-f9602a6ff48f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nIn the given code snippet `malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t))`, the vulnerability could arise if the size of `hipStream_t` is not properly controlled or if the return value of `malloc` is not checked for null (which indicates that the memory allocation failed).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc` for null. If `malloc` returns null, it means that the memory allocation failed. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash your program.\n\n2. Be careful with the size of the memory you are trying to allocate. If the size is user-controlled or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to buffer overflows or memory corruption.\n\n3. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written such as `calloc` or `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nhipStream_t* ptr = (hipStream_t*) malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    // use ptr\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we check if `malloc` returns null and handle the error appropriately. We also make sure to free the allocated memory after we are done using it to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet uses the `malloc` function which is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8b3dafb8-0638-3694-b795-359e9b135c43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of the block is specified by the `size` parameter. If the allocation is successful, the function returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any data.\n\nA vulnerability can occur when the `size` parameter passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities, including buffer overflows, integer overflows, and memory corruption. These vulnerabilities can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, cause a denial of service, or leak sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and limit the `size` parameter passed to `malloc`.\n- Check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the allocation was successful.\n- Initialize the allocated memory to a known value using `memset` or similar function.\n- Use a safe version of `malloc` that includes built-in error checking and validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to safely use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size == 0 || size > SIZE_MAX) {\n        // Invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize memory to zero\n    memset(ptr, 0, size);\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: for `malloc`\n- `string.h`: for `memset`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8bc4e90d-5a0c-3eb1-b8d8-ed8492565120",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6452cde6-3578-338c-9f25-c3dbafadf6c1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an attacker, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust user input for memory allocation without validation. Ensure that the size of the memory to be allocated is within acceptable limits.\n\n2. Error handling: Always check if `malloc()` was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`.\n\n3. Use safer functions: If possible, use safer alternatives to `malloc()` like `calloc()`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define MAX_WIDTH 1000\n\nvoid function(size_t width) {\n    if (width > MAX_WIDTH) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(width);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use ptr\n\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check if the width is greater than a predefined maximum value. If it is, we handle the error and return. Then we call `malloc()` and check if the returned pointer is `NULL`. If it is, we handle the error and return. Finally, we use the allocated memory and free it when we're done.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the standard C library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
