            {
              "id": "2f7ffec1-a36a-33bc-b4cd-0d792ca81a1e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is a common source of vulnerabilities due to its potential to cause buffer overflow. This function copies a specified number of characters from one string to another. If the source string is longer than the specified length, `strncpy` does not append a null character to the destination string. This can lead to buffer overflow if the destination string is used in a context that expects it to be null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null character. You should also ensure that the source string is not longer than the specified length. If the source string is longer, you should truncate it or handle the overflow in a way that does not compromise the security of your program.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with the `strlcpy` function, which takes the size of the destination buffer and ensures that it is null-terminated. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <bsd/string.h>\n\n// ...\n\nstrlcpy(servaddr.sun_path, name, sizeof(servaddr.sun_path));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n- `bsd/string.h`: This library provides the `strlcpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "695c5f89-0f7b-37f6-991e-1929a5eae79b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an attacker, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust user input for memory allocation without validation. Ensure that the size is within the expected range.\n\n2. Error handling: Always check if `malloc()` was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`.\n\n3. Avoid integer overflow: If the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated, ensure that the calculation does not overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\n// Bad code\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\n\n// Good code\nif(size > MAX_SIZE || size < 0) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    char *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\n    if(buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
