            {
              "id": "56d428d5-5dbc-346f-8f0a-5887669cd9fa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `allocsize` parameter is controlled by the user or can be manipulated, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n- Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input blindly.\n- Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, it means that the memory allocation failed.\n- Avoid using `malloc` with a size of 0. This can lead to undefined behavior.\n- Use `calloc` instead of `malloc` when possible. `calloc` initializes the allocated memory to zero, reducing the risk of using uninitialized memory.\n- Free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Check if allocsize is a reasonable size\nif (allocsize > 0 && allocsize <= MAX_ALLOC_SIZE) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(allocsize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37863cd8-e6a4-35b7-ac6f-a5e6e2c8966f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an attacker, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust user input for memory allocation without validation. Ensure that the size is within the expected range.\n\n2. Error handling: Always check if `malloc()` was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`.\n\n3. Avoid integer overflow: If the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated, ensure that the calculation does not overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\n// Bad code\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\n\n// Good code\nif(size > MAX_SIZE || size < 0) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    char *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\n    if(buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
